# Test: a published container port must also be allowed by the operator's
#       firewall policy. With a base chain at the forward hook whose policy is
#       drop and no term that accepts the forwarded connection, a client
#       beyond the router gets no answer and `show doctor` reports
#       doctor-container-port-blocked for the port entry, with the term to
#       add. Once that term is committed, the same client gets the
#       container's line and the finding is gone.
# Spec: spec-container-3-network, "Published ports", Wiring Test row
#       container-port-blocked; umbrella AC-14, the policy part
# QUESTION: does the operator's forward policy decide whether a published
#   port is reachable from beyond the router, and does doctor say so with the
#   term that fixes it?
# VALIDATES: the network plugin's port-blocked check
#   (internal/plugins/container/network/policy.go parseForwardChains,
#   ForwardChain.blocks, portPolicy.findings, acceptingTerm), the record the
#   apply makes for it (apply.go install), its registration as a doctor check
#   of the container plugin (register.go), and that a reload which changes
#   only the firewall section reaches the plugin and clears the finding.
# PREVENTS: a published port that opens the firewall by itself, a blocked port
#   doctor is silent about, a fix line the policy does not obey, and a finding
#   that stays after the term is committed.
# Why not a unit test: the unit tests prove the judgment over a parsed config
#   and the text of the fix. Only a real daemon, kernel and container show
#   that the kernel drops the forwarded connection under that policy, that the
#   term doctor names is the one that lets it through, and that the finding
#   follows a firewall-only reload.
#
# Method: the test store gives a container whose name starts with "serving"
# the probe's run-serving mode (storeseam_zetest.go): it listens on TCP port
# 7070 inside the container and answers every connection with one fixed line.
# The port is published as $PORT2 on the router. The client runs beyond the
# router: in a network namespace of its own, joined to the router by a veth
# pair with an address on each end, connecting to the router's address on
# that link, so its connection is translated at prerouting and then crosses
# the forward hook.
#
# The policy: one inet table with one base chain at the forward hook, policy
# drop. From the start it holds the term that accepts the replies by
# connection state, so the only thing missing is the term for the client's
# packets. allowed.conf is running.conf plus that term, written as the fix
# of the finding gives it: protocol tcp, destination port 7070 (the container
# port, the destination after translation), output interface zcn-lan.
#
# The control inside the test: while the client beyond the router is blocked,
# the router itself reaches the published port (step 12). That connection is
# translated at the output hook and never crosses the forward hook, so it
# shows the container serves and the port is translated, and that the forward
# policy alone is what stops the client.
#
# The probe's adopt mode writes allowed.conf over running.conf and sends
# `request reload`, which answers once every plugin applied the file.
#
# caps: sys-admin for the container and the namespace, net-admin for the
# bridge, the links and the nft tables.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,sys-admin
option=exclusive:group=container
option=timeout:value=240s
option=env:var=ze.test.container.store:value=/run/ze-container-test-$PORT
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT
tmpfs=running.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT;
		}
	}
}
interface {
	backend kernel
}
firewall {
	backend kernel
	table wan {
		family inet
		chain forward {
			type filter
			hook forward
			priority 0
			policy drop
			term replies {
				from {
					connection-state established,related
				}
				then {
					accept
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
container {
	network lan {
		prefix 10.88.0.0/24
	}
	instance serving {
		image probe
		network lan {
		}
		port web {
			public-port $PORT2
			container-port 7070
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF
tmpfs=allowed.conf:terminator=EOF_ALLOWED
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT;
		}
	}
}
interface {
	backend kernel
}
firewall {
	backend kernel
	table wan {
		family inet
		chain forward {
			type filter
			hook forward
			priority 0
			policy drop
			term replies {
				from {
					connection-state established,related
				}
				then {
					accept
				}
			}
			term container_serving_web {
				from {
					protocol tcp
					destination-port 7070
					output-interface zcn-lan
				}
				then {
					accept
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
container {
	network lan {
		prefix 10.88.0.0/24
	}
	instance serving {
		image probe
		network lan {
		}
		port web {
			public-port $PORT2
			container-port 7070
		}
	}
}
EOF_ALLOWED

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-containerprobe reset /run/ze-container-test-$PORT:exit=0

# The client's namespace, and the veth pair that joins it to the router.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ip netns add zeblk$PORT:exit=0
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ip link add zbo$PORT type veth peer name zbi$PORT netns zeblk$PORT:exit=0
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ip address add 172.31.251.1/24 dev zbo$PORT:exit=0
cmd=foreground:seq=5:exec=ip link set dev zbo$PORT up:exit=0
cmd=foreground:seq=6:exec=ip -n zeblk$PORT address add 172.31.251.2/24 dev zbi$PORT:exit=0
cmd=foreground:seq=7:exec=ip -n zeblk$PORT link set dev zbi$PORT up:exit=0

cmd=background:seq=8:exec=ze start running.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.

# Barrier: the container wrote its report, so it holds its link and its
# listener is bound.
cmd=foreground:seq=9:exec=ze-containerprobe networked /run/ze-container-test-$PORT serving 10.88.0.2/24 10.88.0.1 1500:timeout=45s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=probe: eth0 holds 10.88.0.2/24
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL

# The port is translated: the plugin's table holds its term.
cmd=foreground:seq=10:exec=ze cli -c "show firewall ruleset container":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=port_serving_web

# The policy blocks it: the client beyond the router gets no answer.
cmd=foreground:seq=11:exec=ip netns exec zeblk$PORT ze-containerprobe blocked 172.31.251.1 $PORT2:timeout=15s:exit=0
expect=stdout:pattern=probe: the connection to 172\.31\.251\.1:[0-9]+ got no answer
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL

# The control: the router itself reaches the same port, through the output
# hook, so the container serves and only the forward policy stops the client.
cmd=foreground:seq=12:exec=ze-containerprobe published 172.31.251.1 $PORT2:timeout=60s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=probe: the container answered
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL

# Doctor reports the blocked port, its chain, and the term to add.
cmd=foreground:seq=13:exec=ze cli -c "show doctor | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains="code": "doctor-container-port-blocked"
expect=stdout:contains=container serving port web is published and the firewall policy blocks it: chain forward of firewall table wan is at the forward hook with policy drop
expect=stdout:contains=output-interface zcn-lan or destination-address 10.88.0.2,
expect=stdout:contains=set firewall table wan chain forward term container_serving_web from protocol tcp
expect=stdout:contains=set firewall table wan chain forward term container_serving_web from destination-port 7070
expect=stdout:contains=set firewall table wan chain forward term container_serving_web from output-interface zcn-lan
expect=stdout:contains=set firewall table wan chain forward term container_serving_web then accept

# The operator adds the term and commits it.
cmd=foreground:seq=14:exec=ze-containerprobe adopt running.conf allowed.conf:timeout=90s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=probe: the daemon reloaded its configuration
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL

# The same client now gets the container's line.
cmd=foreground:seq=15:exec=ip netns exec zeblk$PORT ze-containerprobe published 172.31.251.1 $PORT2:timeout=60s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=probe: connected to 172.31.251.1:
expect=stdout:contains=probe: the container answered
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL

# The finding is gone.
cmd=foreground:seq=16:exec=ze cli -c "show doctor | json":exit=0
reject=stdout:contains=doctor-container-port-blocked

# A port no container publishes is not translated and not forwarded: the
# router's own stack refuses it, from the same outside namespace.
cmd=foreground:seq=17:exec=ip netns exec zeblk$PORT ze-containerprobe refused 172.31.251.1 $PORT3:timeout=15s:exit=0
expect=stdout:pattern=probe: the connection to 172\.31\.251\.1:[0-9]+ was refused
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL

# The daemon stops in order and the container ends; the namespace goes, and
# the veth pair with it.
cmd=stop:seq=18:name=daemon:signal=term
cmd=foreground:seq=19:exec=ze-containerprobe gone /run/ze-container-test-$PORT serving:timeout=50s:exit=0
expect=stdout:contains=probe: its control group is gone
reject=stdout:contains=probe: FAIL
cmd=foreground:seq=20:exec=ip netns delete zeblk$PORT:exit=0
